#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Append 85 extra law pairs to upgrade_j.jsonl to reach 200"""
import json, pathlib

OUT = pathlib.Path('/workspace/data_upgrades')

extra = [
    ("What is the tort of deceit (fraud)?", "Deceit requires: (1) a false representation; (2) known to be false or made without belief in its truth or recklessly (Derry v Peek); (3) intended to be relied upon; (4) actually relied upon by the claimant; (5) causing loss. The claimant recovers all losses flowing directly from the fraud, whether foreseeable or not — a wider measure than contractual damages."),
    ("What is the difference between a lease and a licence?", "A lease grants exclusive possession of land for a term at a rent — creating a proprietary interest binding third parties (Street v Mountford [1985]). A licence merely permits the licensee to be on land without exclusive possession — a personal right only. The distinction matters for security of tenure, protection against eviction, and rights against new owners."),
    ("What is adverse possession?", "Adverse possession (squatter's title) allows a person who has openly, exclusively, and continuously possessed land without the owner's permission for the limitation period (12 years under the Limitation Act 1980; reformed by LRA 2002 for registered land — 10 years, then notice to register) to acquire title to it. The registered land regime makes adverse possession much harder to establish."),
    ("What is a mortgage in law?", "A mortgage is a security interest over property. The mortgagor (borrower) grants security to the mortgagee (lender); if repayment fails, the lender can take possession and sell the property. Equity protects the mortgagor: no clog on the equity of redemption (right to repay and recover free title); extortionate credit bargains may be reopened. The mortgagee owes duties on sale (to obtain best price at the time of sale)."),
    ("What is easement in land law?", "An easement is a right appurtenant to one piece of land (dominant tenement) over another (servient tenement). Requirements (Re Ellenborough Park): there must be dominant and servient tenements; the right must accommodate and serve the dominant tenement; tenements must be owned by different persons; the right must be capable of forming the subject matter of a grant. Examples: right of way, right to light, right to lay pipes."),
    ("What is a covenant in land law?", "A covenant is a promise in a deed. Positive covenants (to do something) do not run with the land at common law (Rhone v Stephens). Restrictive covenants (not to do something) run in equity where they are negative, protect neighbouring land, and the covenantor had notice (Tulk v Moxhay principle). Restrictive covenants are registered; they bind successors with notice. Discharge or modification is possible under s.84 LPA 1925."),
    ("What is the law of landlord and tenant?", "Landlord-tenant law governs leasehold relationships. Landlord obligations: covenant for quiet enjoyment; non-derogation from grant; repair obligations (implied in short residential leases). Tenant obligations: pay rent; not to commit waste; comply with terms; not sublet/assign without consent (if required). Residential tenants have statutory protection against eviction and rent increases. Commercial leases are largely governed by contract."),
    ("What is the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954?", "The LTA 1954 (Part II) gives business tenants a statutory right to renew their lease on termination, on similar terms, unless the landlord establishes a statutory ground for opposition (e.g., redevelopment, own occupation). It fundamentally alters freedom of contract by giving tenants security of tenure. Parties can contract out of Part II (with procedural safeguards). Compensation is payable on non-renewal for some grounds."),
    ("What is a constructive trust?", "A constructive trust arises by operation of law to prevent unconscionable conduct, regardless of the parties' intentions. It is imposed where: a stranger to a trust received trust property with knowledge (receipt); a stranger assisted a breach of trust dishonestly (knowing assistance); or common intention between parties about ownership of property, on which one relied to their detriment (Lloyds Bank v Rosset — threshold for common intention is high)."),
    ("What is the resulting trust?", "A resulting trust arises where equity presumes a person who provided consideration for property intended to retain a beneficial interest. Types: automatic resulting trust (purpose of an express trust fails — surplus results back to settlor); presumed resulting trust (purchase in another's name without gift — contributor has beneficial interest in proportion to contribution — rebutted by presumption of advancement between parent and child or spouses)."),
    ("What is criminal conspiracy?", "Conspiracy (Criminal Law Act 1977) is an agreement between two or more persons to pursue a course of conduct which, if carried out, would amount to or involve the commission of an offence by any one or more of them. It is committed on agreement — no overt act is required. Conspiracy to defraud remains common law. The mens rea requires knowledge of all material circumstances and intention that the offence be committed."),
    ("What is the law on attempts?", "Criminal Attempts Act 1981: a person commits an attempt if they do an act which is more than merely preparatory to the commission of an offence, with intention to commit the full offence. The 'more than merely preparatory' test is objective (Gullefer, Geddes, Jones). Attempting the impossible is an offence (Shivpuri). Mens rea: intention to commit the full offence (recklessness insufficient even where recklessness suffices for the full offence)."),
    ("What is secondary liability in criminal law?", "Accessories (aiders, abettors, counsellors, procurers) are criminally liable for the principal's offence under s.8 Accessories and Abettors Act 1861. Actus reus: assistance or encouragement; mens rea: intentional assistance with knowledge of the principal's circumstances and intention to assist (Jogee [2016] SC — overruled parasitic accessory liability, restored intention as required mens rea for accessories)."),
    ("What is joint enterprise liability post-Jogee?", "R v Jogee [2016]: the Supreme Court overruled previous parasitic accessory liability under which D2 was liable for all foreseeable acts of D1. The correct test: D2 must have intentionally assisted or encouraged the principal's conduct with intent to do so. D2's foresight of the possibility of the principal's act is evidence of intent but not conclusive. The ruling narrowed secondary liability significantly."),
    ("What is the law of theft?", "Theft Act 1968 s1: dishonestly appropriating property belonging to another with the intention of permanently depriving the other of it. Key elements: appropriation (assuming any of the owner's rights — Morris; Gomez; Hinks); property (including confidential information is not property — Oxford v Moss); belonging to another; dishonesty (Ivey v Genting [2017] objective test: did D act dishonestly by the standard of ordinary decent people?); intention permanently to deprive."),
    ("What is fraud under the Fraud Act 2006?", "The Fraud Act 2006 creates a general fraud offence in three ways: fraud by false representation (s2 — dishonestly making a false representation intending to gain or cause loss); fraud by failing to disclose (s3 — legal duty to disclose); fraud by abuse of position (s4 — occupying a position of trust and abusing it dishonestly). No deception is required — the offence is complete on making the representation dishonestly."),
    ("What is money laundering?", "Money laundering is the process of making criminally derived proceeds appear legitimate. The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 (POCA) creates three principal offences: concealing, disguising, converting or transferring criminal property (s327); arranging (s328); acquiring, using or possessing criminal property (s329). The regulated sector has reporting obligations; failure to disclose suspicion is an offence. Maximum sentence: 14 years."),
    ("What is the law of bail?", "Bail Act 1976 creates a right to bail for unconvicted defendants; it may be refused only if substantial grounds exist to believe the defendant will fail to surrender, commit further offences, obstruct justice, or (in serious cases) their own protection. Conditions may be imposed (residence, curfew, reporting). The prosecution can appeal grant of bail; defendants can appeal refusal. Breach of bail conditions can lead to arrest and remand."),
    ("What is the criminal standard of proof?", "The prosecution must prove guilt beyond reasonable doubt — sometimes expressed as the jury being 'sure' of guilt. This is a higher standard than civil (balance of probabilities) because the consequences (imprisonment) are graver. The presumption of innocence is protected by Article 6(2) ECHR. Where reverse burdens are imposed by statute, courts must ensure they are compatible with the presumption of innocence."),
    ("What is contempt of court?", "Contempt of court punishes conduct interfering with the administration of justice: civil contempt (disobedience of a court order — committal or fine); criminal contempt (conduct likely to prejudice proceedings — publications during trials, scandalising the court, juror misconduct). The Contempt of Court Act 1981 creates strict liability for publications creating a substantial risk of serious prejudice to active proceedings."),
    ("What is legal aid?", "Legal aid provides state funding for legal advice and representation for those who cannot afford it. In England and Wales, the Legal Aid Agency administers legal aid under LASPO 2012, which cut civil legal aid significantly, removing most housing, family, welfare benefit, and debt cases. Criminal legal aid remains broader (custodial cases almost always funded). Means and merits tests apply. The justice gap — unmet legal need — is a major policy concern."),
    ("What is the Bribery Act 2010?", "The Bribery Act 2010 (UK) creates four offences: bribing another person (s1); being bribed (s2); bribing a foreign public official (s6); and the corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery (s7). The corporate offence has strict liability but an adequate procedures defence. The Act has extraterritorial reach for UK nationals and companies with a UK connection. Maximum sentence: 10 years. Enforcement by the Serious Fraud Office."),
    ("What is confiscation in criminal law?", "POCA 2002 (Part 2) allows courts to confiscate the proceeds of crime from convicted defendants. The court makes a confiscation order equal to the benefit from criminal conduct, limited to available assets. The 'criminal lifestyle' provisions presume assets are proceeds if the defendant has benefited from general criminal conduct. Failure to pay results in imprisonment in default."),
    ("What is public nuisance?", "Public nuisance is a crime and a tort. As a crime: conduct that materially affects the reasonable comfort and convenience of a class of Her Majesty's subjects. As a tort: a member of the public who suffers special damage (beyond ordinary) may sue. Examples: obstructing the highway, polluting water, dangerous premises. Tort liability requires special damage; the criminal liability protects the public interest."),
    ("What is the law on search and seizure?", "PACE 1984 (UK): police require reasonable grounds for suspicion to stop and search (s1); warrant or statutory power to enter and search premises; powers to seize anything they have reasonable grounds to believe is evidence or liable to be forfeited. PACE Codes of Practice supplement statutory powers. Evidence obtained in breach may be excluded under s78 PACE if its admission would adversely affect the fairness of proceedings."),
    ("What is the Data Protection Act 2018?", "The Data Protection Act 2018 supplements and implements GDPR in UK domestic law. It covers areas GDPR leaves to member states: law enforcement data processing (Part 3 — implementing LED); national security; data processing for purposes outside GDPR scope. The ICO regulates data protection and can impose fines up to £17.5m or 4% of global turnover for the most serious breaches."),
    ("What is the law of limitation?", "Limitation statutes bar claims after a period of time to promote certainty and protect defendants from stale claims. Key periods (UK — Limitation Act 1980): contract and negligence (6 years from accrual of cause of action); personal injury (3 years from date of injury or knowledge — s14); latent damage in negligence (6 or 3 years from knowledge — s14A); land (12 years). Courts have discretion to disapply personal injury limitation (s33)."),
    ("What is security of tenure for residential tenants?", "Assured shorthold tenancies (Housing Act 1988) are the most common form. Landlords can recover possession using s21 notices (no-fault, 2 months' notice after fixed term) or s8 notices (fault grounds — rent arrears, breach). Renters' Reform legislation proposes abolishing s21. Social housing tenants have greater security. The Rent Act 1977 protected tenancies have the strongest security and fair rent provisions."),
    ("What is the Children Act 1989?", "The Children Act 1989 is the foundation of England and Wales child law. Principles: the welfare of the child is paramount (s1); the no-order principle (orders only if better than no order); the welfare checklist (s1(3)); and non-delay principle. Key orders: child arrangements orders (previously residence/contact); special guardianship; care orders (transferring parental responsibility to local authority); emergency protection."),
    ("What is parental responsibility?", "Parental responsibility (Children Act 1989 s3): all the rights, duties, powers, and responsibilities a parent of a child has by law. Mothers automatically have PR; married fathers have PR automatically; unmarried fathers acquire PR by agreement, registration on birth certificate, or court order. Step-parents, guardians, and others can acquire PR. Multiple people can hold PR simultaneously."),
    ("What is adoption law?", "Adoption creates a permanent, legal parent-child relationship, extinguishing the birth parents' legal parental relationship. Adoption and Children Act 2002: must be in the child's best interests (welfare paramount — s1 checklist); court makes a placement order authorising adoption agency to place child; adoption order made when child has lived with prospective adopters 10+ weeks. Birth parents' consent is required or dispensed with if child's welfare demands."),
    ("What is the matrimonial law on divorce?", "The Divorce, Dissolution and Separation Act 2020 (UK) introduced no-fault divorce from April 2022 — replacing the prior fault-based or separation grounds. One or both parties simply apply on the basis that the marriage has broken down irretrievably; no supporting facts required. A 20-week period then elapses before a conditional order; the final order follows 6 weeks later."),
    ("What is ancillary relief / financial remedies on divorce?", "On divorce, courts distribute assets between spouses using the Matrimonial Causes Act 1973 discretionary factors: financial needs, resources, contributions, standard of living, ages, duration, disability, and s25A — achieving clean break. The starting point for long marriages is equal division (White v White — no discrimination in valuing homemaking). Non-matrimonial assets (pre-acquired, gifts) may be ringfenced."),
    ("What is the law of copyright infringement?", "Copyright infringement occurs when a restricted act (copying, adaptation, communication, performance, rental) is done without licence in respect of a substantial part of the work. Substantive copying is determined qualitatively (not just quantitatively). Fair dealing defences apply for research, criticism, news reporting, and parody (CDPA 1988). Remedies: injunction, damages (or account of profits), and delivery up."),
    ("What is the doctrine of res judicata?", "Res judicata ('the matter has been decided') bars re-litigation of the same issue between the same parties. Cause of action estoppel: a cause of action that has been decided cannot be relitigated. Issue estoppel: a specific issue decided in earlier proceedings cannot be relitigated. The Henderson v Henderson rule extends this to matters that ought to have been raised in earlier proceedings."),
    ("What is abuse of process?", "Courts have an inherent jurisdiction to stay proceedings as an abuse of process: where re-litigation is vexatious or oppressive (Henderson v Henderson); where the defendant cannot receive a fair trial (R v Looseley — entrapment); where prosecution would compromise the integrity of the judicial process; or where there has been a deliberate manipulation of the court's processes. Abuse results in stay of proceedings."),
    ("What is the law on directors' duties — business judgment rule?", "While English law has no formal business judgment rule, s172 CA 2006 requires directors to act in good faith to promote success of the company for shareholders' benefit, having regard to stakeholder interests. Courts give deference to good faith commercial decisions — they will not second-guess business decisions absent bad faith, irrationality, or conflict of interest. The standard of care in s174 is both objective and subjective (higher of actual and reasonable director standard)."),
    ("What is corporate governance?", "Corporate governance refers to the systems and processes by which companies are directed and controlled. The UK Corporate Governance Code applies to premium-listed companies on a 'comply or explain' basis. It covers board composition (diversity, independence, skills), audit and remuneration committees, shareholder engagement, risk management, and succession planning. The Walker Report (banking) and Stewardship Code (institutional investors) supplement it."),
    ("What is the rule against perpetuities?", "The rule against perpetuities prevents interests in property vesting beyond a set period. At common law: lives in being plus 21 years. The Perpetuities and Accumulations Act 2009 introduced a fixed 125-year perpetuity period for new instruments. The rule prevents wealth being tied up indefinitely, ensuring property eventually becomes freely available. Charitable trusts are exempt from the rule."),
    ("What is the law on passing off — damage element?", "The third limb of the Classic Trinity (Reckitt v Borden) requires actual or likely damage to goodwill — blurring (diluting the distinctiveness of the claimant's mark), tarnishment (associating the claimant's mark with inferior products), or direct diversion of trade. Mere inconvenience or confusion without goodwill damage is insufficient. Damage can be presumed where the misrepresentation is likely to divert customers."),
    ("What is the tort of breach of statutory duty?", "Where a statute imposes a duty on a defendant, breach causing foreseeable harm may give rise to a private law action if: Parliament intended to confer a private right; the claimant is in a class protected by the statute; the harm is of the type the statute was designed to prevent; and the breach caused the harm. A tort of breach of statutory duty is separate from negligence and is not available where Parliament intended only regulatory enforcement."),
    ("What is the principle of non-refoulement?", "Non-refoulement (Refugee Convention Art 33; CAT Art 3; ECHR Art 3) prohibits returning a person to a country where they face a real risk of persecution, torture, or inhuman or degrading treatment. It is absolute under ECHR Art 3 (Soering v UK). The Refugee Convention permits departure where the person poses a danger to national security or has been convicted of a serious crime — but ECHR Art 3 permits no exceptions."),
    ("What is the law of passing off — international aspects?", "Passing off requires goodwill in the jurisdiction where the claim is brought. For international businesses, goodwill must be established in the UK — a purely foreign reputation without UK customers is insufficient (Anheuser-Busch v Budejovicky Budvar). Internet trade complicates territoriality. The Paris Convention and TRIPS require states to protect against unfair competition, and WTO panels resolve trade mark disputes."),
    ("What are anti-suit injunctions?", "An anti-suit injunction restrains a party from bringing or continuing foreign proceedings that are in breach of an exclusive jurisdiction clause, where the foreign litigation is oppressive or vexatious, or where a party seeks to undermine English proceedings. Granted as in personam against the party (not the foreign court). Post-Brexit, EU member state courts may not recognise English anti-suit injunctions restraining EU proceedings."),
    ("What is the Rome Regulations in private international law?", "Rome I Regulation (contractual obligations — EU 593/2008): parties may choose the governing law; absent choice, law of the country of habitual residence of the characteristic performer applies. Rome II Regulation (non-contractual obligations — EU 864/2007): law of the country where damage occurs (lex loci damni) governs, unless parties agree otherwise. Post-Brexit UK has retained Rome I and II as domestic law."),
    ("What is the Brussels Regulation in private international law?", "The Brussels Regulation (EU 1215/2012 — Recast) allocates jurisdiction among EU member state courts and requires recognition and enforcement of judgments between them. General rule: courts of the defendant's domicile have jurisdiction. Special rules: contract claims in courts of place of performance; tort claims in courts of place of harmful event. Post-Brexit, Brussels Regulation no longer applies to UK courts."),
    ("What is the law of insolvency — claw-back provisions?", "On insolvency, antecedent transactions may be set aside: transactions at an undervalue within 2 years (5 if connected party); preferences within 6 months (2 years if connected party); transactions defrauding creditors (no time limit — IA 1986 s423); floating charges for past consideration within 12 months (2 years if connected party — only valid for new consideration). Insolvency practitioners apply to court to restore assets to the estate."),
    ("What is the law on private nuisance — defences?", "Defences to private nuisance: statutory authority (if statute authorises the activity and interference is inevitable consequence); prescription (20 years' continuous tortious use — Sturges v Bridgman); contributory negligence (may reduce damages). Not defences: coming to the nuisance (Sturges v Bridgman — claimant cannot be denied remedy because they moved to an existing nuisance); that the activity benefits the public."),
    ("What is ADR — the role of mediation?", "Mediation is a voluntary, confidential process where a neutral mediator helps parties reach a mutually acceptable resolution. The mediator facilitates communication and exploration of interests rather than imposing a decision. Parties retain control over outcome. Courts increasingly encourage mediation pre-litigation (CPR r1.4; Ungley Order); unreasonable refusal to mediate may result in adverse costs orders (Halsey v Milton Keynes)."),
    ("What is the CISG (Vienna Convention)?", "The United Nations Convention on International Sale of Goods (1980) provides a uniform contract law for international commercial sales between parties in contracting states (97 states). It covers formation, conformity, risk of loss, remedies, and damages. Parties may exclude it. It does not cover consumer sales, services, or IP. The CISG uses the concept of 'fundamental breach' to trigger the right to avoid the contract."),
    ("What is the United Nations Charter and the use of force?", "The UN Charter (Art 2(4)) prohibits the threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or political independence of states. Exceptions: self-defence against an armed attack (Art 51 — anticipatory self-defence is disputed) and Security Council authorisation (Chapter VII — for threats to peace, breaches of peace, or acts of aggression). The right to self-defence is individually and collectively exercisable; imminent threat doctrine is debated."),
    ("What is the law of evidence — similar fact evidence?", "Similar fact evidence (propensity evidence) shows a defendant has committed similar acts on other occasions. Criminal Justice Act 2003 (ss101–106): bad character evidence is admissible if it satisfies a gateway, including where it is important explanatory evidence, relevant to an important matter in issue between prosecution and defendant (propensity), or relevant to defendant's truthfulness. Courts must exclude if prejudicial effect outweighs probative value."),
    ("What is the right to a fair trial under Article 6 ECHR?", "Article 6 guarantees the right to a fair and public hearing within a reasonable time by an independent and impartial tribunal established by law. Specific rights in criminal proceedings: presumption of innocence; right to be informed of the charge; adequate time and facilities to prepare; legal assistance (free if necessary); examine witnesses; and interpreter if needed. Some rights may be limited for national security but overall fairness must be maintained."),
    ("What is the law on dishonesty post-Ivey?", "The Supreme Court in Ivey v Genting Casinos [2017] overruled the subjective limb of Ghosh, holding that dishonesty is objective: the standard is that of ordinary decent people. The court first asks what the defendant's actual state of knowledge and belief was, then objectively assesses whether their conduct was dishonest by that standard. The subjective limb (did the defendant realise ordinary people would regard it as dishonest?) was abolished."),
    ("What is the law on gross negligence manslaughter?", "Gross negligence manslaughter (Adomako [1994]): the defendant owes a duty of care to the victim; that duty is breached; the breach causes death; and the breach is so gross that it should be judged criminal. The jury decides whether the breach warrants criminal liability — circularity criticised. Doctors, railway operators, and care home managers have been convicted. Distinguished from corporate manslaughter (statutory — CMCHA 2007)."),
    ("What is the law on provocation/loss of control?", "Coroners and Justice Act 2009 replaced provocation with the partial defence of loss of control for murder. Three requirements: D loses self-control; the loss is due to a qualifying trigger (fear of serious violence from V, or circumstances of an extremely grave character causing D to have a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged); and a person of D's sex and age with a normal degree of tolerance would have reacted similarly. Sexual infidelity cannot be a qualifying trigger."),
    ("What is the law on diminished responsibility?", "Homicide Act 1957 s2 (as amended by CJA 2009): diminished responsibility reduces murder to manslaughter where the defendant's ability to understand their conduct, form a rational judgement, or exercise self-control was substantially impaired by an abnormality of mental functioning arising from a recognised medical condition that caused or was a significant contributory factor in the killing. Voluntary intoxication alone is excluded."),
    ("What is the law on consent in criminal law?", "Consent may negate the actus reus of some offences (assault, battery) or operate as a defence. Consent cannot authorise actual bodily harm inflicted for purposes not in the public interest (R v Brown — sadomasochism; compare tattooing, surgery). Sexual offences: consent requires agreement with capacity and freedom to choose (Sexual Offences Act 2003 s74); presumptions of absence of consent in certain situations."),
    ("What is the law of evidence — character evidence?", "Evidence of a defendant's bad character is prima facie inadmissible (prejudicial) but admissible through CJA 2003 gateways. Good character evidence is always admissible for the defendant (Vye direction — goes to credibility and propensity). Co-defendants may adduce each other's bad character (s101(1)(e)) to undermine each other. Previous convictions are admissible for propensity where they make it more likely the defendant committed the offence."),
    ("What is the law on strict liability torts?", "The principle in Rylands v Fletcher (strict liability for escape of dangerous things from non-natural use of land) is the main non-negligence strict liability tort in English law. The Consumer Protection Act 1987 imposes strict product liability. Employers are vicariously liable for employees' torts regardless of fault. The Animals Act 1971 imposes strict liability for damage done by animals of a dangerous species or where owner knows of the animal's dangerous tendency."),
    ("What is the law on negligent misstatement?", "Pure economic loss from negligent misstatements is recoverable where there is a special relationship of proximity: Hedley Byrne v Heller [1964] — the defendant voluntarily assumes responsibility for the accuracy of advice and the claimant reasonably relies on it to their detriment. The extended Hedley Byrne principle (Henderson v Merrett Syndicates) applies to assumption of responsibility for services as well as statements."),
    ("What is the law on pure economic loss in negligence?", "Pure economic loss (financial loss not consequent on physical damage to the claimant's person or property) is generally not recoverable in negligence to avoid opening floodgates of indeterminate liability. Exceptions: negligent misstatement (Hedley Byrne); where the defendant assumed responsibility and claimant relied upon it (White v Jones — solicitor's negligence in not drafting will before testator died). Policy limitations remain strict."),
    ("What is the law of trespass to the person?", "Trespass to the person comprises assault (apprehension of immediate unlawful force), battery (intentional and direct application of unlawful force — Collins v Wilcock), and false imprisonment (complete restriction of freedom of movement without lawful justification — Meering v Grahame White). Trespass is actionable per se (no proof of damage required). Consent, lawful arrest, and self-defence are defences."),
    ("What is defamation — honest opinion defence?", "The honest opinion defence (Defamation Act 2013 s3) protects statements of opinion if: it indicated (explicitly or implicitly) that it was opinion; it was based on fact (existent at the time or in a privileged statement); an honest person could have held the opinion on those facts. The defendant need not prove the facts are true — only that a basis existed. It replaces the old fair comment defence."),
    ("What is the Reynolds privilege / responsible journalism defence?", "The Defamation Act 2013 s4 codifies and replaces Reynolds privilege, creating a publication on matter of public interest defence. The defendant must believe the publication was in the public interest and must have acted responsibly in verifying and publishing. Responsible journalism factors (Reynolds ten-point list) remain relevant to what is responsible. The defence is unavailable if the claimant requests that a correction be published and the defendant refuses."),
    ("What is the law on justification/truth in defamation?", "The Defamation Act 2013 s2 replaced the common law defence of justification with the defence of truth. If the defendant proves the defamatory imputation is substantially true, the defence succeeds — truth is an absolute defence regardless of the defendant's motivation. Where a statement contains several charges, those not proved true must not materially injure the claimant's reputation beyond those that are proved true."),
    ("What is qualified privilege in defamation?", "Qualified privilege protects publications made without malice on occasions of duty or interest where the publisher and recipient have a corresponding duty or interest in the communication. Examples: employment references, reports to police, professional communications within an organisation. Malice destroys the privilege. Statutory qualified privilege (Defamation Act 1996, Sch 1) covers fair and accurate reports of legislative and judicial proceedings."),
    ("What is the law on privacy and misuse of private information?", "The tort of misuse of private information (Naomi Campbell v MGN) arises where the claimant has a reasonable expectation of privacy in the information and the defendant's publication is not justified by a balancing of competing interests (especially ECHR Art 8 vs Art 10). Injunctions (super-injunctions, anonymised) prevent publication; damages are awarded for distress and loss of control of private information."),
    ("What is Article 10 freedom of expression?", "Article 10 ECHR protects freedom of expression — ideas and information that offend, shock, or disturb as well as those that are well-received. Restrictions must be prescribed by law, necessary in a democratic society, and proportionate to a legitimate aim (protection of reputation, national security, preventing disorder, protecting rights of others). The press has heightened protection; prior restraint is subject to the highest scrutiny."),
    ("What is the margin of appreciation in ECHR law?", "The margin of appreciation is the discretion given to contracting states by the ECtHR in implementing ECHR rights, reflecting that national authorities are better placed to assess local needs and conditions. The margin varies: narrow where rights are absolute (Art 3), wider where states must balance competing interests (Arts 8-11) and where no European consensus exists on a moral issue. Courts balance universality with subsidiarity."),
    ("What is positive obligation under ECHR?", "Positive obligations require states not merely to refrain from interfering with rights but to take active steps to protect them. Examples: Art 2 — duty to investigate deaths, operational duty to protect identified individuals at risk; Art 3 — duty to investigate allegations of torture; Art 8 — duty to protect private life from interference by private actors. Positive obligations create public law duties for police, prosecutors, and public bodies."),
    ("What is State immunity in international law?", "State immunity (State Immunity Act 1978, UK) prevents foreign states from being sued in domestic courts without their consent — the traditional absolute doctrine evolved into restrictive immunity: states are not immune for commercial (acta jure gestionis) acts but immune for sovereign (acta jure imperii) acts. Exceptions: commercial transactions, employment, personal injury, property in the forum, and waiver. Immunity applies to state officials for official acts."),
    ("What is diplomatic immunity?", "Diplomatic immunity (Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations 1961) grants diplomatic agents full immunity from criminal and civil jurisdiction of the receiving state, preventing them from being prosecuted or sued. Immunity is waived only by the sending state. The immunity attaches to the function, not the person. Serious abuses (using diplomatic pouches for drugs) have prompted debate; the sending state is obliged to prosecute the diplomat at home."),
    ("What is the concept of proportionality in sentencing?", "Proportionality requires that punishment be commensurate with the seriousness of the offence — neither too lenient nor excessively harsh. It derives from the retributive principle that punishment should match the criminal's desert. In England and Wales, the Sentencing Council guidelines set offence-specific ranges based on culpability and harm. Mandatory sentences (mandatory life for murder; minimum terms for firearms) constrain proportionality."),
    ("What is community order in sentencing?", "A community order (Criminal Justice Act 2003) is a non-custodial sentence combining requirements: unpaid work (40-300 hours); curfew with electronic monitoring; supervision (probation); attendance centre; prohibited activities; residence requirement; mental health or drug/alcohol treatment; rehabilitation activity requirement. Community orders aim to rehabilitate and may be more effective than short custodial sentences in reducing reoffending."),
    ("What is a suspended sentence?", "A suspended sentence (Criminal Justice Act 2003 s189) is a custodial sentence with its period of immediate imprisonment suspended for a supervision period, during which requirements may be imposed. If the defendant commits a further offence or breaches requirements during the suspension period, the original sentence is usually activated. It combines the deterrent effect of a custodial sentence with the rehabilitative benefits of community supervision."),
    ("What is victim personal statement in criminal proceedings?", "A victim personal statement (VPS) allows victims to describe the impact of a crime in their own words before sentencing. The court must consider it when sentencing. The VPS is read aloud (if the victim requests) in the Crown Court. It gives victims a voice in the process, though courts must impose proportionate sentences regardless of the victim's wishes regarding severity."),
    ("What is the Civil Procedure Rules?", "The Civil Procedure Rules (CPR 1998) govern civil litigation in England and Wales, implementing the Woolf reforms. The overriding objective (r1.1) is to deal with cases justly and at proportionate cost. Key features: active case management by courts; pre-action protocols; disclosure obligations; witness statements exchanged in advance; case management conferences; small claims, fast, and multi-track allocation based on value and complexity."),
    ("What is disclosure in civil litigation?", "Disclosure (CPR Part 31) requires parties to identify and allow inspection of documents that are or have been in their control that are relevant to the issues. Standard disclosure covers documents the party relies on, and those adversely affecting or supporting another party's case. Electronic disclosure involves complex management of ESI. The Disclosure Pilot (CPR PD 57AD — Business and Property Courts) replaced standard disclosure with tailored, proportionate models."),
    ("What is without prejudice privilege?", "Without prejudice privilege protects genuine settlement negotiations from being disclosed in litigation. Communications marked 'without prejudice' (or made in a genuine attempt to settle) cannot be put before the court to show admissions or offers. The privilege is joint — both parties must consent to waive. Exceptions: where the communications themselves are a ground of the action (fraud or perjury in the negotiations)."),
    ("What is pre-action protocol in civil litigation?", "Pre-action protocols (PAPs) set out steps parties must take before commencing litigation to exchange information, attempt ADR, and narrow issues. They cover specific areas (personal injury, clinical negligence, defamation, construction disputes). Non-compliance can result in adverse costs orders, sanctions, or stays. PAPs reflect the CPR overriding objective of proportionality and encourage resolution without litigation."),
    ("What is Part 36 offer in civil litigation?", "A Part 36 offer (CPR Part 36) is a formal settlement offer with automatic cost consequences if it is not beaten at trial. Defendant's offer: if claimant fails to beat the offer at trial, claimant pays defendant's costs from expiry of the offer and suffers interest and indemnity costs consequences. Claimant's offer: if claimant beats the offer, defendant pays indemnity costs and enhanced interest on the judgment sum and damages. Part 36 strongly incentivises settlement."),
    ("What is expert evidence in civil litigation?", "Under CPR Part 35, expert evidence is restricted to that reasonably required to resolve proceedings. Experts owe an overriding duty to the court, not the instructing party. Single joint experts are encouraged for smaller cases. Experts must set out their reasoning, assumptions, and the range of opinion. Court permission is required to adduce expert evidence. Hot-tubbing (concurrent evidence by experts together) is used in complex commercial cases."),
]

